---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import Term from '../../components/Term.astro';
import SourceBadge from '../../components/SourceBadge.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import Checklist from '../../components/Checklist.astro';
import { crisis } from '../../lib/site';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const checked = '2026-11-11';

const papers = [
  "The patient's <strong>Aadhaar card</strong>, plus a few photocopies",
  "The <strong>mobile phone linked to the patient's Aadhaar</strong> (it receives the booking OTP)",
  'Any <strong>referral letter</strong> from the doctor or hospital who sent you',
  '<strong>All earlier reports</strong> in date order: blood tests, scans (with the CD or films), biopsy reports, and discharge summaries',
  'A written <strong>list of every medicine</strong> the patient takes now, with doses',
  '<strong>Ayushman Bharat card</strong>, ration card or income certificate, if you have them (needed for some financial help)',
  'Two or three <strong>passport-size photos</strong> of the patient and of the attendant',
  'A <strong>file folder</strong> to keep everything together, and a pen and notebook',
];

const bag = [
  'Phone charger and a power bank',
  'Water bottle and simple food for long waits',
  'A shawl or light blanket (waiting areas and nights can be cold)',
  "The patient's regular medicines for the whole trip, plus a few extra days",
  'Some cash in small notes, in case card or UPI payment fails',
  'Phone numbers of family at home, written on paper as well as saved in the phone',
];

const sources = [
  { title: 'Appointment system at AIIMS (notice)', publisher: 'AIIMS New Delhi, Computer Facility', url: 'https://aiims.edu/images/pdf/notice/Digital%20AIIMS/appointment%20system-white.pdf', note: 'Undated; describes the system up to 2017. Used for how ORS booking, UHID and counters work.' },
  { title: 'Online Registration System (ORS)', publisher: 'Government of India', url: 'https://ors.gov.in', note: 'Official booking portal.' },
  { title: "Inauguration of 'Infosys Foundation Vishram Sadan' Dharamshala at the National Cancer Institute, AIIMS", publisher: 'Infosys (press release, 2021)', url: 'https://www.infosys.com/newsroom/press-releases/2021/inauguration-vishram-sadan-dharamshala.html' },
  { title: 'PM to inaugurate the Infosys Foundation Vishram Sadan at National Cancer Institute in Jhajjar campus of AIIMS New Delhi', publisher: 'Press Information Bureau, Government of India (2021)', url: 'https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1765166' },
  { title: 'CSR impact assessment reports 2022–23', publisher: 'Infosys', url: 'https://www.infosys.com/investors/reports-filings/documents/csr-impact-assessment-reports2022-23.pdf', note: 'Mentions admission documents and a tariff at the Vishram Sadan.' },
  { title: 'Delhi Urban Shelter Improvement Board', publisher: 'Government of NCT of Delhi', url: 'https://dusib.delhi.gov.in', note: 'Runs night shelters in Delhi.' },
];
---
<Page
  title="Coming to Delhi for treatment"
  eyebrow="Find care · Handbook"
  lede="A practical guide for families travelling to Delhi for cancer care: what to bring, how to get an appointment, where to stay, and how to get through the first days."
  updated={checked}
  toc={[
    { id: 'first', label: 'Before you read on' },
    { id: 'papers', label: 'Papers to bring' },
    { id: 'appointment', label: 'Getting an appointment' },
    { id: 'day', label: 'On the day' },
    { id: 'stay', label: 'Where to stay' },
    { id: 'money', label: 'Help with costs' },
    { id: 'you', label: 'Looking after yourself' },
    { id: 'sources', label: 'Sources' },
  ]}
>
  <SourceBadge status="summarised" sources={['AIIMS New Delhi', 'Government of India ORS', 'PIB']} lastChecked={checked} />

  <h2 id="first">Before you read on</h2>
  <p>
    Travelling to a big hospital in an unfamiliar city is hard, especially when someone you love is unwell. You don't
    need to read all of this at once. Start with <a href="#papers">the papers to bring</a>; that is the step that saves
    the most trouble later.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Hospital procedures change often.</strong> Everything here was checked on the date at the top of the page,
      but please confirm timings, fees and rules with the hospital itself. Where we couldn't confirm a detail from an
      official source, we've said so instead of guessing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="no-print"><button class="btn btn-secondary" type="button" onclick="window.print()">Print the checklists</button></p>

  <h2 id="papers">Papers to bring</h2>
  <p>Doctors at a new hospital will want to see everything that has happened so far. Gather it before you leave home:</p>
  <Checklist label="Papers to bring" items={papers} />
  <Disclosure title="Why bring the old reports and scans, if they will test again?">
    <p>
      Earlier reports help the new doctors understand what has already been found and done, and can save time. The new
      hospital may still repeat some tests. If a biopsy was done elsewhere, ask that hospital or lab how to collect the
      biopsy slides or blocks in case the new hospital asks to review them.
    </p>
  </Disclosure>

  <h3>For the journey and the waiting</h3>
  <Checklist label="Things to carry" items={bag} />

  <h2 id="appointment">Getting an appointment</h2>
  <p>
    AIIMS New Delhi takes appointments through the government's <strong>Online Registration System (ORS)</strong> at
    <a href="https://ors.gov.in" rel="noopener">ors.gov.in</a>. Some other government hospitals use ORS too; check whether
    yours is listed. According to AIIMS, this is how it works:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>On the ORS website, choose the hospital and the department, then a date.</li>
    <li>Verify the patient with their <strong>Aadhaar number</strong>. An OTP is sent to the mobile number linked to that Aadhaar. People without Aadhaar can register using a mobile number instead.</li>
    <li>A new patient who verifies with Aadhaar also receives a <Term id="uhid">UHID</Term>, the patient's hospital ID number for all future visits.</li>
    <li>A confirmation SMS is sent to the registered mobile number with the details of the visit, such as reporting time and room.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If you can't book online, AIIMS also describes registration counters and self-service kiosks at the hospital for
    new appointments.
  </p>
  <Disclosure title="Which department should we book?">
    <p>
      If you have a referral letter, it usually names the department or the kind of specialist the patient should see.
      If you are unsure, ask the doctor who referred you, or ask at the hospital's registration or enquiry counter.
      We can't advise which department is right for a particular patient.
    </p>
  </Disclosure>
  <Disclosure title="AIIMS has a cancer campus in Jhajjar, Haryana">
    <p>
      The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is part of AIIMS New Delhi but is located on its Jhajjar campus in Haryana,
      outside Delhi. Before travelling, check which campus your appointment is at, and plan the journey between them.
    </p>
  </Disclosure>

  <h2 id="day">On the day</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Go early.</strong> Big government hospitals are very busy, and queues for registration can be long.</li>
    <li><strong>Get the patient's hospital card or registration slip</strong> (often called the <Term id="parchi">parchi</Term>) and keep it safe in your file. You will usually need it at every visit.</li>
    <li><strong>Before leaving each counter or room, ask: "Where do we go next, and when?"</strong> Write the answer in your notebook.</li>
    <li><strong>Write down what the doctor says</strong>, including the names of tests and the date of the next visit. It's fine to ask the doctor to repeat or write something down.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep every receipt and report</strong> in date order. You will need them for follow-ups and for financial help.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="stay">Where to stay</h2>
  <p>
    Finding a place to stay near a big hospital is one of the hardest parts for families coming from far away. These
    are the main options. Availability changes a lot, especially in winter, so try to arrange something before you travel.
  </p>
  <h3>1. Ask the hospital first</h3>
  <p>
    Large hospitals usually have a <strong>medical social service</strong> or social worker department that helps
    patients' families with practical problems, which can include finding a place to stay. Ask for it at the enquiry
    counter.
  </p>
  <h3>2. Hospital rest houses and dharamshalas</h3>
  <p>
    Several hospitals have rest houses or a <Term id="dharamshala">dharamshala</Term> nearby for patients and their
    families, often at low cost. Ask the hospital which ones serve its patients and how to apply.
  </p>
  <p>
    For patients at the <strong>National Cancer Institute, AIIMS (Jhajjar campus)</strong>, the Infosys Foundation
    built the <strong>Vishram Sadan</strong> next to the hospital. When it opened in 2021 it was described as free of
    cost, with about 800 beds. A later Infosys report mentions documents such as the OPD card and ID, and a tariff,
    for admission. <strong>Please confirm current charges and the process with the hospital before relying on it.</strong>
  </p>
  <h3>3. Government night shelters</h3>
  <p>
    The Delhi Urban Shelter Improvement Board (DUSIB) runs free night shelters, called <Term id="rain-basera">rain basera</Term>,
    across Delhi, and in some seasons sets up shelters near large hospitals for patients' families. News reports
    describe them as very crowded in winter. For current locations and the helpline, see the
    <a href="https://dusib.delhi.gov.in" rel="noopener">DUSIB website</a>. <em>We couldn't confirm the current helpline
    number from an official source, so we haven't listed it here yet.</em>
  </p>
  <h3>4. Private guest houses and dharamshalas</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask other families in the waiting area; they often know what's nearby and what it costs.</li>
    <li>Agree the price, and what it includes, <strong>before</strong> you pay or move in.</li>
    <li>Be careful of anyone who approaches you outside the hospital offering rooms, faster appointments or "contacts" for money. Appointments are made only through the hospital's official system.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="money">Help with costs</h2>
  <p>
    Treatment at government hospitals is heavily subsidised, but tests, medicines, travel and stay can still add up.
    Help exists, including the government's Ayushman Bharat scheme, other government funds for poor patients, and
    charities. Ask the hospital's social service department what help its patients can apply for, and keep copies of
    every bill and report.
  </p>
  <p>Our full guide to financial help is being written and will appear here.</p>

  <h2 id="you">Looking after yourself</h2>
  <p>
    Caregivers often stop eating properly, sleep on floors and push through exhaustion. You matter too. If there are
    other family members, take turns, so everyone gets rest. Eat something even when you don't feel like it.
  </p>
  <div class="callout warm">
    <p>
      If it all feels too much, you can talk to someone, free:
      {crisis.lines.map((l, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', or '}<a href={`tel:${l.tel}`}><strong>{l.number}</strong></a> ({l.name.split(' (')[0]})</>)}.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
  <p class="small muted">All checked on {new Date(checked).toLocaleDateString('en-IN', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' })}.</p>
  <ol class="sources small">
    {sources.map((s) => (
      <li>
        <a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.title}</a>. {s.publisher}.{s.note && <> <span class="muted">{s.note}</span></>}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p class="small">
    Something here wrong or out of date? Please tell us; see <a href={url('/editorial-policy#corrections')}>corrections</a>.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .sources { padding-left: 1.25em; }
  .sources li { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
